<h1 class="article-title">Liepāja SEZ Authority Signs Contract for Karosta Canal Remediation Works</h1>
<div class="block_text mt-5 mb-4">
<div class="row">
<div class="col"><div class="col-12 output"><p>The Liepāja Special Economic Zone Authority has signed a contract with Eko osta Ltd for remediation of the Karosta Canal at the Port of Liepāja and removal of historically contaminated sediments. Under the contract, remediation must begin after the Karosta Canal remediation programme has been prepared and approved, but no later than 1 April 2023, and must be completed by 30 November 2023. Approximately 164,000 cubic metres of contaminated sediment are to be dredged at a total contract value of EUR 3,497,260.00.</p><p>The Liepāja SEZ Authority is continuing the project “Remediation Works at the Historically Contaminated Site of the Karosta Canal in the Port of Liepāja” to reduce the adverse impacts of historical sediment pollution on the environment, human health and the ecological condition of the Baltic Sea. The remediation will remove sediments from the canal bed that fail to meet regulatory standards and contain 400 tonnes of hazardous chemicals. The project is financed under the Norwegian Financial Mechanism 2014–2021 programme “Climate Change Mitigation, Adaptation and Environment”.</p><p>“This long-planned and unique project will enable the Liepāja SEZ Authority to achieve several objectives: address environmental and ecological issues in the Baltic Sea region, remove the canal from HELCOM’s list of Baltic Sea ‘hot spots’ and create opportunities for further business development in the Karosta Canal basin and adjacent areas,” emphasised Liepāja SEZ CEO Uldis Hmieļevskis.</p><p>Project manager Ivo Koliņš noted that the entire public would benefit from the project, as it would help achieve the objectives and measures of the Helsinki Commission (HELCOM) Baltic Sea Action Plan: restoring the Baltic Sea to good ecological status, with natural biodiversity and functioning ecosystems, a clean, healthy and productive marine environment and sustainable use of marine resources for current and future generations. As a result, Liepāja residents would no longer face risks arising from historical pollution, and both the ecological condition of the sea and overall environmental quality would improve significantly.</p><p>The project continues earlier efforts to clean the Karosta Canal at the Port of Liepāja. With EU Cohesion Fund financing, the first phase of the project, “Clean-up of the Historically Contaminated Site of the Karosta Canal at the Port of Liepāja”, was completed by 2015. Large quantities of human-made waste, including scrap metal, concrete structures and timber, were removed and disposed of over 78 hectares of the canal. Around 60,000 cubic metres of polluted sediments were also removed from the most heavily contaminated 10-hectare section and deposited at a specially equipped site at the eastern end of the canal. The canal’s overall ecological condition improved considerably: concentrations of petroleum products and heavy metals in sediments fell several times over, although they still did not meet the requirements of Cabinet of Ministers regulations.</p><p>The Karosta Canal clean-up at the Port of Liepāja is a project of European significance, and its outcomes must reflect best practice in implementing environmental protection projects. One project partner, the Norwegian Institute for Water Research (NIVA), is participating to share examples of similar remediation work in Norway and provide knowledge to Latvian specialists responsible for maintaining the achieved improvements in the future.</p><p>The Karosta Canal remediation works at the Port of Liepāja will be implemented under the project “Remediation Works at the Historically Contaminated Site of the Karosta Canal in the Port of Liepāja” by the Liepāja Special Economic Zone Authority in cooperation with the Norwegian Institute for Water Research and the association “Baltijas krasti”.</p><p>The project implementation period runs from 1 August 2021 to 31 December 2023.</p><p>The project budget is EUR 5,117,647.05, including EUR 4,350,000.00 in co-financing from the Norwegian Financial Mechanism.</p><p> </p><p>“Working together for a green Europe”</p></div>
